<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Libya</h1><p class="st-meta">1969 CE – present · capital Tripoli</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A North African republic founded by the 1969 coup of Muammar Gaddafi, split since the 2011 civil war between rival governments in Tripoli and the east.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-libya">Kingdom of Libya</a>, a constitutional and hereditary monarchy independent since 24 December 1951 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idris-of-libya">King Idris I</a>, was overthrown on 1 September 1969 by military officers led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muammar-gaddafi">Muammar Gaddafi</a>, in a coup known as the Al Fateh Revolution. They created a republic, and Gaddafi was referred to as the Brother Leader and Guide of the Revolution.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Gaddafi dominated Libya for four decades. Political dissent was made illegal under Law 75 of 1973, and Revolutionary Committees carried out widespread surveillance. In March 1977 the country became the Great Socialist People's Libyan Arab Jamahiriya, with power formally passed to the General People's Committees. Oil income paid for programmes such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-man-made-river">Great Man-Made River</a> and for arms purchases. Libya was put under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanctions">sanctions</a> after the Lockerbie bombing, which killed 270 people. Protests began on 15 February 2011, and Gaddafi was captured and killed in Sirte on 20 October 2011; at least 30,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libyans">Libyans</a> died in the war.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>After 2011 the National Transitional Council handed power to the elected General National Congress, and a second civil war began in May 2014 between rival parliaments in Tripoli and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tobruk">Tobruk</a>. A permanent ceasefire was signed on 23 October 2020. Since March 2022 two governments have functioned at once, and the international community recognises the unity government. Floods on 10 September 2023 killed more than 5,900 people in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derna">Derna</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/libya.jpg" alt="Libya" width="330" height="165"><figcaption>Image: Various The SVG code is valid . This vector image was created with a text editor . · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Libya.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1969 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tripoli</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Muammar Gaddafi, Mustafa A.G. Abushagur, Ali Zeidan, Fayez Al-Sarraj, Abdul Hamid Dbeibeh</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam, small Ibadi communities</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic; Berber languages also spoken</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/libya">Libya</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>State of Libya, Libyan Arab Republic, Libyan Arab Jamahiriya</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1998&amp;focus=libya" aria-label="Libya on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5018 983 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Libya lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5338 1239L5338 1239"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5338 1239h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5338 1239h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5338 1239h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Tripoli. <a href="/atlas?year=1998&amp;focus=libya">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
